      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The Genesee Valley Canal opened in 1840, turning Piffard into a trade hub with grain and lumber transport.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        2,
        7
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/piffard_new_york",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "The Abbey of the Genesee, a Trappist monastery founded in 1951 on a 570‑acre farm, produces famous Monks’ Bread.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        3,
        25
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia",
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Piffard%2C_New_York",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Piffard’s historic Westerly house, built in 1850, blends Greek Revival and Italianate styles and is on the National Register.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        18,
        30
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1",
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/piffard_new_york",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "In the late 1800s, a sawmill built in 1860 grew into a stave and barrel factory by 1874, fueling local industry.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
